event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":2034,"item_id":"fc_0c7496a9be2f26dc016adb1af2c9dc8191a4d8b01fd6d23e38","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"PROOF_CANDIDATE\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"Let n,m be positive integers; let u:R^n×(0,∞)→R^m be jointly smooth and q∈R^m have Euclidean norm 1. Assume |u(x,r)|=1 for every (x,r); for each (x,r) there is a real a(x,r) with u_tt−Δ_xu=a(x,r)u; and on every compact time interval J⊂(0,∞), u(x,r)=q outside a compact spatial set C_J, uniformly for r∈J. With e(x,r)=|u_t|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|∂_{x_j}u|² and E(r)=∫_{R^n}e(x,r)dx (Lebesgue measure; Euclidean vector norms and Frobenius spatial-gradient square), prove e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable for every r>0, E is differentiable at every r>0, and E'(r)=0. No endpoint assertion at 0.\\\",\\\"proof\\\":\\\"Fix an arbitrary r₀>0 and choose a compact interval J contained in (0,∞) with r₀ in its interior. By the support hypothesis there is a compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C_J and r∈J. For each such x the function of r is constant on J, so u_t(x,r)=0 there; likewise its spatial derivatives vanish on the open set R^n\\\\\\\\C_J. By smoothness and continuity of the derivatives, those derivatives also vanish on the boundary of C_J. Thus for every r∈J the functions u_t(·,r), u_{x_j}(·,r), and e(·,r) vanish outside C_J. The function e(·,r) is continuous and bounded on the compact set C_J, hence measurable and Lebesgue integrable on R^n (it vanishes off a compact set). This proves the required integrability, since r₀ was arbitrary.\\\\n\\\\nChoose a closed box B=[−R,R]^n containing C_J. For all r∈J, E(r)=∫_B e(x,r)dx. The function (x,r)↦e(x,r) is smooth. Its time derivative e_t is continuous on the compact set B×J, hence uniformly continuous there. For h sufficiently small with r₀+h∈J, the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus gives, for every x∈B, (e(x,r₀+h)−e(x,r₀))/h=∫₀¹ e_t(x,r₀+θh)dθ. Uniform continuity implies these difference quotients converge uniformly in x∈B to e_t(x,r₀) as h→0. Since B has finite Lebesgue measure, the difference between their integrals and ∫_B e_t(x,r₀)dx is bounded by |B| times the uniform error, and tends to zero. Consequently E is differentiable at r₀ and E'(r₀)=∫_B e_t(x,r₀)dx=∫_{R^n}e_t(x,r₀)dx.\\\\n\\\\nWrite u_j=∂_{x_j}u and u_{jj}=∂_{x_j}²u. Differentiating the finite sum defining e, using smoothness to commute mixed partials, gives e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_j·∂_{x_j}u_t. Each term is continuous and supported in C_J at time r₀. For each j, the scalar function u_t·u_j is smooth and compactly supported in x, and its x_j derivative is (∂_{x_j}u_t)·u_j+u_t·u_{jj}. Its integral over R^n is zero: integrate first in x_j, where the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus gives zero because the function vanishes for sufficiently large positive and negative x_j, and then integrate the other variables. Fubini applies since this derivative is continuous with compact support. Therefore ∫(∂_{x_j}u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_{jj}. Summing yields E'(r₀)=2∫_{R^n}u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)dx.\\\\n\\\\nIt remains to identify the scalar in the normal-acceleration hypothesis. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in time gives u·u_t=0 and |u_t|²+u·u_{tt}=0. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in x_j gives u·u_j=0 and |u_j|²+u·u_{jj}=0. Summing the latter identities gives u·Δ_xu=−Σ_j|u_j|². Hence u·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². On the other hand the hypothesis says u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a(x,r₀)u; dotting with u and using |u|²=1 shows a(x,r₀)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². In particular the acceleration is a scalar multiple of u, while u·u_t=0, so pointwise u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(x,r₀)u_t·u=0. The integrand in the displayed expression for E'(r₀) is therefore zero everywhere, proving E'(r₀)=0. As r₀ was arbitrary, all conclusions hold for every r>0.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport, Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/LocallyIntegrable.lean:622–625 (candidate mathlib declaration; not yet source-probed against the pinned checkout).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"Could justify integrability of e(·,r): establish continuity, Borel/Lebesgue measure instance, and compact support from the locally uniform support assumption. The natural-language proof instead directly uses boundedness and compact support. Confirm full pinned signature and applicability before registering as M.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"One-variable fundamental theorem of calculus and Fubini's theorem for Lebesgue integration (standard external calculus/measure results; no particular source theorem is being asserted as verified).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"FTC is used for the parameter difference-quotient identity and for the integral of the compactly supported x_j derivative to vanish. Fubini is used to integrate that derivative one coordinate at a time; its continuous compact support gives absolute integrability, and the box/whole-space integrals agree by vanishing outside the support. If formalized via mathlib, inspect the exact pinned declarations and full hypotheses; these uses can alternatively be expanded using compact-box integration and elementary one-dimensional calculus.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Differentiation of an integral over a fixed compact box under a continuous parameter derivative (standard parameter-integral theorem; the proof above supplies its compact-domain argument rather than relying on an unexpanded citation).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"On B×J, e_t is uniformly continuous; the FTC representation makes the difference quotients converge uniformly on B, and B has finite measure. Thus the integral difference quotients converge to ∫_B e_t. The common box is valid only because the support is uniform over J.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Candidate mathlib source search: intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/FundThmCalculus.lean; candidate integration-by-parts family integral_deriv_mul_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/IntegrationByParts.lean; and integral_bilinear_hasDerivAt_right_eq_neg_left_of_integrable in Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/LineDeriv/IntegrationByParts.lean.\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"These are source-probe leads only, not theorem applications in this proof. Their exact signatures/domain and hypotheses must be checked before use; the proof needs only compact-support integration by parts, which it derives directly using FTC and Fubini.\\\"}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":[\\\"The arbitrary time r₀ is strictly positive; choose J with r₀ in its interior, so no claim or limiting argument at t=0 is involved.\\\",\\\"A compact C_J need not have nonempty interior and the proof does not require it; derivatives vanish on its open complement and on its boundary by continuity.\\\",\\\"u itself need not be compactly supported: it equals the fixed unit vector q off C_J. Only its derivatives and the energy density are compactly supported.\\\",\\\"The spatial term is the sum of componentwise squared derivatives, so Δ_x and the integration-by-parts sum match the stipulated Frobenius/Hilbert–Schmidt convention.\\\",\\\"No regularity of a(x,r) is required; its value is identified pointwise by dotting the given acceleration equation with u.\\\",\\\"Both n and m are positive integers, as assumed; all sums are finite.\\\"],\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"This is a natural-language proof candidate, not Audit A or Lean acceptance. The candidate mathlib names have not been verified by a pinned full-signature source probe.\\\",\\\"The standard FTC and Fubini facts should be source-registered and their exact applications reviewed in a later source-completion/Audit A stage if the NL0 package treats them as external inputs; their conditions are established in the argument as stated.\\\"]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":2035,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0c7496a9be2f26dc016adb1af2c9dc8191a4d8b01fd6d23e38","call_id":"call_08e991b408a2482593481b11430e99be","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"PROOF_CANDIDATE\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"Let n,m be positive integers; let u:R^n×(0,∞)→R^m be jointly smooth and q∈R^m have Euclidean norm 1. Assume |u(x,r)|=1 for every (x,r); for each (x,r) there is a real a(x,r) with u_tt−Δ_xu=a(x,r)u; and on every compact time interval J⊂(0,∞), u(x,r)=q outside a compact spatial set C_J, uniformly for r∈J. With e(x,r)=|u_t|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|∂_{x_j}u|² and E(r)=∫_{R^n}e(x,r)dx (Lebesgue measure; Euclidean vector norms and Frobenius spatial-gradient square), prove e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable for every r>0, E is differentiable at every r>0, and E'(r)=0. No endpoint assertion at 0.\\\",\\\"proof\\\":\\\"Fix an arbitrary r₀>0 and choose a compact interval J contained in (0,∞) with r₀ in its interior. By the support hypothesis there is a compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q whenever x∉C_J and r∈J. For each such x the function of r is constant on J, so u_t(x,r)=0 there; likewise its spatial derivatives vanish on the open set R^n\\\\\\\\C_J. By smoothness and continuity of the derivatives, those derivatives also vanish on the boundary of C_J. Thus for every r∈J the functions u_t(·,r), u_{x_j}(·,r), and e(·,r) vanish outside C_J. The function e(·,r) is continuous and bounded on the compact set C_J, hence measurable and Lebesgue integrable on R^n (it vanishes off a compact set). This proves the required integrability, since r₀ was arbitrary.\\\\n\\\\nChoose a closed box B=[−R,R]^n containing C_J. For all r∈J, E(r)=∫_B e(x,r)dx. The function (x,r)↦e(x,r) is smooth. Its time derivative e_t is continuous on the compact set B×J, hence uniformly continuous there. For h sufficiently small with r₀+h∈J, the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus gives, for every x∈B, (e(x,r₀+h)−e(x,r₀))/h=∫₀¹ e_t(x,r₀+θh)dθ. Uniform continuity implies these difference quotients converge uniformly in x∈B to e_t(x,r₀) as h→0. Since B has finite Lebesgue measure, the difference between their integrals and ∫_B e_t(x,r₀)dx is bounded by |B| times the uniform error, and tends to zero. Consequently E is differentiable at r₀ and E'(r₀)=∫_B e_t(x,r₀)dx=∫_{R^n}e_t(x,r₀)dx.\\\\n\\\\nWrite u_j=∂_{x_j}u and u_{jj}=∂_{x_j}²u. Differentiating the finite sum defining e, using smoothness to commute mixed partials, gives e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_j·∂_{x_j}u_t. Each term is continuous and supported in C_J at time r₀. For each j, the scalar function u_t·u_j is smooth and compactly supported in x, and its x_j derivative is (∂_{x_j}u_t)·u_j+u_t·u_{jj}. Its integral over R^n is zero: integrate first in x_j, where the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus gives zero because the function vanishes for sufficiently large positive and negative x_j, and then integrate the other variables. Fubini applies since this derivative is continuous with compact support. Therefore ∫(∂_{x_j}u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_{jj}. Summing yields E'(r₀)=2∫_{R^n}u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)dx.\\\\n\\\\nIt remains to identify the scalar in the normal-acceleration hypothesis. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in time gives u·u_t=0 and |u_t|²+u·u_{tt}=0. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in x_j gives u·u_j=0 and |u_j|²+u·u_{jj}=0. Summing the latter identities gives u·Δ_xu=−Σ_j|u_j|². Hence u·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². On the other hand the hypothesis says u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a(x,r₀)u; dotting with u and using |u|²=1 shows a(x,r₀)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². In particular the acceleration is a scalar multiple of u, while u·u_t=0, so pointwise u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(x,r₀)u_t·u=0. The integrand in the displayed expression for E'(r₀) is therefore zero everywhere, proving E'(r₀)=0. As r₀ was arbitrary, all conclusions hold for every r>0.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport, Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/LocallyIntegrable.lean:622–625 (candidate mathlib declaration; not yet source-probed against the pinned checkout).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"Could justify integrability of e(·,r): establish continuity, Borel/Lebesgue measure instance, and compact support from the locally uniform support assumption. The natural-language proof instead directly uses boundedness and compact support. Confirm full pinned signature and applicability before registering as M.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"One-variable fundamental theorem of calculus and Fubini's theorem for Lebesgue integration (standard external calculus/measure results; no particular source theorem is being asserted as verified).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"FTC is used for the parameter difference-quotient identity and for the integral of the compactly supported x_j derivative to vanish. Fubini is used to integrate that derivative one coordinate at a time; its continuous compact support gives absolute integrability, and the box/whole-space integrals agree by vanishing outside the support. If formalized via mathlib, inspect the exact pinned declarations and full hypotheses; these uses can alternatively be expanded using compact-box integration and elementary one-dimensional calculus.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Differentiation of an integral over a fixed compact box under a continuous parameter derivative (standard parameter-integral theorem; the proof above supplies its compact-domain argument rather than relying on an unexpanded citation).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"On B×J, e_t is uniformly continuous; the FTC representation makes the difference quotients converge uniformly on B, and B has finite measure. Thus the integral difference quotients converge to ∫_B e_t. The common box is valid only because the support is uniform over J.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Candidate mathlib source search: intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/FundThmCalculus.lean; candidate integration-by-parts family integral_deriv_mul_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/IntegrationByParts.lean; and integral_bilinear_hasDerivAt_right_eq_neg_left_of_integrable in Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/LineDeriv/IntegrationByParts.lean.\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"These are source-probe leads only, not theorem applications in this proof. Their exact signatures/domain and hypotheses must be checked before use; the proof needs only compact-support integration by parts, which it derives directly using FTC and Fubini.\\\"}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":[\\\"The arbitrary time r₀ is strictly positive; choose J with r₀ in its interior, so no claim or limiting argument at t=0 is involved.\\\",\\\"A compact C_J need not have nonempty interior and the proof does not require it; derivatives vanish on its open complement and on its boundary by continuity.\\\",\\\"u itself need not be compactly supported: it equals the fixed unit vector q off C_J. Only its derivatives and the energy density are compactly supported.\\\",\\\"The spatial term is the sum of componentwise squared derivatives, so Δ_x and the integration-by-parts sum match the stipulated Frobenius/Hilbert–Schmidt convention.\\\",\\\"No regularity of a(x,r) is required; its value is identified pointwise by dotting the given acceleration equation with u.\\\",\\\"Both n and m are positive integers, as assumed; all sums are finite.\\\"],\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"This is a natural-language proof candidate, not Audit A or Lean acceptance. The candidate mathlib names have not been verified by a pinned full-signature source probe.\\\",\\\"The standard FTC and Fubini facts should be source-registered and their exact applications reviewed in a later source-completion/Audit A stage if the NL0 package treats them as external inputs; their conditions are established in the argument as stated.\\\"]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

