 E'(r₀)=2∫_{R^n}u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)dx.\\\\n\\\\nIt remains to identify the scalar in the normal-acceleration hypothesis. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in time gives u·u_t=0 and |u_t|²+u·u_{tt}=0. Differentiating |u|²=1 twice in x_j gives u·u_j=0 and |u_j|²+u·u_{jj}=0. Summing the latter identities gives u·Δ_xu=−Σ_j|u_j|². Hence u·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². On the other hand the hypothesis says u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a(x,r₀)u; dotting with u and using |u|²=1 shows a(x,r₀)=−|u_t|²+Σ_j|u_j|². In particular the acceleration is a scalar multiple of u, while u·u_t=0, so pointwise u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(x,r₀)u_t·u=0. The integrand in the displayed expression for E'(r₀) is therefore zero everywhere, proving E'(r₀)=0. As r₀ was arbitrary, all conclusions hold for every r>0.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Continuous.integrable_of_hasCompactSupport, Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/LocallyIntegrable.lean:622–625 (candidate mathlib declaration; not yet source-probed against the pinned checkout).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"Could justify integrability of e(·,r): establish continuity, Borel/Lebesgue measure instance, and compact support from the locally uniform support assumption. The natural-language proof instead directly uses boundedness and compact support. Confirm full pinned signature and applicability before registering as M.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"One-variable fundamental theorem of calculus and Fubini's theorem for Lebesgue integration (standard external calculus/measure results; no particular source theorem is being asserted as verified).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"FTC is used for the parameter difference-quotient identity and for the integral of the compactly supported x_j derivative to vanish. Fubini is used to integrate that derivative one coordinate at a time; its continuous compact support gives absolute integrability, and the box/whole-space integrals agree by vanishing outside the support. If formalized via mathlib, inspect the exact pinned declarations and full hypotheses; these uses can alternatively be expanded using compact-box integration and elementary one-dimensional calculus.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Differentiation of an integral over a fixed compact box under a continuous parameter derivative (standard parameter-integral theorem; the proof above supplies its compact-domain argument rather than relying on an unexpanded citation).\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"On B×J, e_t is uniformly continuous; the FTC representation makes the difference quotients converge uniformly on B, and B has finite measure. Thus the integral difference quotients converge to ∫_B e_t. The common box is valid only because the support is uniform over J.\\\"},{\\\"result\\\":\\\"Candidate mathlib source search: intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/FundThmCalculus.lean; candidate integration-by-parts family integral_deriv_mul_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt in Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/IntervalIntegral/IntegrationByParts.lean; and integral_bilinear_hasDerivAt_right_eq_neg_left_of_integrable in Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/LineDeriv/IntegrationByParts.lean.\\\",\\\"use_and_applicability_obligations\\\":\\\"These are source-probe leads only, not theorem applications in this proof. Their exact signatures/domain and hypotheses must be checked before use; the proof needs only compact-support integration by parts, which it derives directly using FTC and Fubini.\\\"}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":[\\\"The arbitrary time r₀ is strictly positive; choose J with r₀ in its interior, so no claim or limiting argument at t=0 is involved.\\\",\\\"A compact C_J need not have nonempty interior and the proof does not require it; derivatives vanish on its open complement and on its boundary by continuity.\\\",\\\"u itself need not be compactly supported: it equals the fixed unit vector q off C_J. Only its derivatives and the energy density are compactly supported.\\\",\\\"The spatial term is the sum of componentwise squared derivatives, so Δ_x and the integration-by-parts sum match the stipulated Frobenius/Hilbert–Schmidt convention.\\\",\\\"No regularity of a(x,r) is required; its value is identified pointwise by dotting the given acceleration equation with u.\\\",\\\"Both n and m are positive integers, as assumed; all sums are finite.\\\"],\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"This is a natural-language proof candidate, not Audit A or Lean acceptance. The candidate mathlib names have not been verified by a pinned full-signature source probe.\\\",\\\"The standard FTC and Fubini facts should be source-registered and their exact applications reviewed in a later source-completion/Audit A stage if the NL0 package treats them as external inputs; their conditions are established in the argument as stated.\\\"]}\"}","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read an explicitly frozen task document by ID; no other local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no te